                <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
                <div class="mt-16">
                  <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
                    Common Blacktop Sealing Mistakes
                  </h2>

                  <p>
                    Blacktop sealer is cheap material and a full day of labour, which makes the mistakes that force a redo expensive in the only currency that matters.
                  </p>

                  <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Stir</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Sealer separates in the pail, with the solids settling into a dense layer at the bottom. Poured straight off the top, the first section of driveway gets mostly water and the last of the pail is unusable. Mix thoroughly with a drill paddle before starting and stir again partway through.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Over Oil Stains</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Sealer will not bond to petroleum, so every oil spot becomes a bare patch that shows through within weeks. Degrease the stains, rinse, let them dry, and prime them with an oil-spot primer before the sealer goes anywhere near them.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying One Heavy Coat</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        A thick coat skins over on top and stays soft underneath, then cracks or peels through the first winter. Two thin coats, each spread out with a squeegee and back-brushed, gives a better colour and far longer life. Aim for roughly a gallon per 80 to 100 square feet per coat.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Opening the Driveway Too Early</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Sealer is dry to the touch long before it has cured, and turning tyres on a soft coat leaves permanent scuffs and power-steering marks. Keep foot traffic off for 24 hours and vehicles off for 48, longer in cool or humid weather.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Closing -->
                <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
                  <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
                  <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                    <strong class="text-white">Blacktop driveway sealer</strong> is one of the best-value maintenance jobs a homeowner can take on, provided the driveway is a candidate for it. A faded, lightly cracked surface will get years back from a coat; one with alligator cracking or sunken areas needs repair first.
                  </p>
                  <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
                    The process is what determines the result: fill the cracks, degrease and clean, wait for a dry stretch above 50°F, stir the pail properly, and lay down two thin coats. Then keep the cars off for a full 48 hours and expect three or four years before the next round.
                  </p>
                </div>

